  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A few showers over the region overnight.\n\nBroad, low-level streamline convergence with its N/S axis near\nthe RT 219 corridor in Western PA has resulted in some showers\nsince earlier this afternoon. Not real warm to the west this\nevening, but western areas have dewpoints into the lower 60s.\ncompared across most of the Susquehanna Vly. Also while winds\nare rather weak, directional shear is there on the 00Z soundings\nthis evening.\n\nSurface convergence becomes more focused over the eastern half\nof the forecast area for tomorrow. Slightly higher dew points\nwill allow for instability to be a little higher than it is\ntoday, possibly approaching 1000 J/kg in a few locations. Though\nupper level forcing looks to be weak, we would expect to see at\nleast a few showers and thunderstorms develop during the\nafternoon. This location of the fcst activity is different than\nwhat was noted the other day. Location could shift more as we\nnear Sunday aft.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Turning more humid and unsettled early next week\nwith the highest probability for showers and thunderstorms \nMonday afternoon and evening and again on Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight.\n\nMain chance for widespread showers and storms will be Tuesday\ninto Wednesday. Fcst dewpoints not all that high for late July,\nand there has been a slight downward trend with fcst dewpoins \nover the last few days.\n\nMain things supporting showers and storms is a deepening low,\nand a large scale pattern that supports directional shear to\nbe present into next week. Models today do show a pattern change\nout toward day 10, a change from recent model runs the last few\ndays. Thus will have to seen if this trend holds.\n\nEarlier discussion below.\n\nMinor modifications for this period that will be highlighted by\na warm front moving across western PA Monday afternoon/evening,\nfollowed by a rather potent/digging mid/upper level shortwave,\nthat will support a sub-995 mb sfc low over the Lower Glakes and\nWestern NY Tuesday afternoon and evening. Latest model runs have\ntrended several hours or so slower compared to runs 24 hours\nago.  \n\nThe timing of the favorable lift associated with the thermally \nindirect, left front quad of the 80+ KT jet max moving across\nthe Ohio Valley, WVA and southern PA Tuesday afternoon and \nevening will help to enhance a low level lee trough and \nconvection along it. Scattered strong to severe TSRA are likely \nTue afternoon and evening over at least the eastern half of the\nCWA with a second line of TSRA likely along the cold front\nacross NW PA Tuesday afternoon.",
